tion \"Analyze the environment and report the content of any keys related to ingredients or USDA data. If no keys are found, explain that the input is missing.\", :reads [:available-keys], :writes [:env-report], :output-schemas {:env-report :string}}] [:final {:keys [:env-report]}]]\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - INSTRUCTION:\n\nCalculate per-serving nutritional data for the three seasonal menu items using the attached ingredient list and USDA reference sheet. Required metrics for each: total calories, protein, carbs, and fats. Must identify all major allergens per standard guidelines. Adjust for specific serving sizes: grain bowl is one container, side salad is 150g, and protein snack pack is two units per serving. Generate formatted nutrition labels for each suitable for packaging sleeves. Do not scale bulk prep quantities; final output must reflect per-serving consumer metrics only.\n\nREADS: (none)\nWRITES: :result\nMCP-TOOLS: (none)\nBROWSER-TOOLS: (none)\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 4 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Transformation (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: This task is a deterministic calculation of nutrition data (calories, protein, carbs, fats) based on a fixed reference sheet and serving sizes. The 'Transformation' subtree recommends using :code for deterministic mappings to preserve fidelity and run efficiently. The instruction specifically says 'Do not scale bulk prep quantities; final output must reflect per-serving consumer metrics only', which is a classic mechanical reshape and calculation best handled by a pure function in :code.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Transformation is mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes. ALWAYS use :code (not :llm) for deterministic transformations — preserves fidelity, costs nothing, runs in ms; :llm for transformation produces non-deterministic output. For per-item independent transformations, :map-each isolates per-item failures. ALWAYS declare :output-schemas on transformation output and :input-schemas on downstream :reads so shape mismatch fails at the boundary, not deep in the consumer. Composes into etl-pipeline (sequential transform chain), parallel-sum (map-reduce transformation), sequential-pipeline shells, and map-reduce shells. Avoid for transformations that need judgment (semantic rephrasing / style shift — use :llm) or for unstructured input (extract first).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given input data + a target shape spec, emit the input restructured to the target shape\n  - natural fit anywhere a tree needs to change data shape without semantic interpretation\n  - outputs are typically a single restructured value or a vector of per-item transformations\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** implement transformations in :code (NOT :llm) when the mapping is deterministic — preserves byte-for-byte fidelity, costs zero tokens, runs in milliseconds (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the transformation rule can be expressed as a pure function over the input\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:output] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:output (mapv (fn [r] (select-keys r [:a :b])) (:input inputs))})}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for per-item independent transformations over a vector, use :map-each rather than a single batch :code call when the per-item work is non-trivial — supports per-item failure isolation (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: items can fail independently AND partial success has value\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :records :as :record :writes [:transformed]} [:code {:reads [:record] :writes [:transformed] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:transformed (assoc (:record inputs) :stamp \"x\"