    "## Suggested patterns from corpus\n\nThese are concrete EXAMPLES retrieved from the seed corpus based on classification of your task. Each example includes:\n  - WHY the candidate fits (reranker reasoning)\n  - The pattern's prose summary (seed `:summary`)\n  - Capabilities it provides\n  - Proven STRENGTHS — traits observed to work, each with a worked-example DSL snippet you can adapt\n  - Observed WEAKNESSES — failure modes others hit, with the recommended fix\n  - Representative uses where this pattern has shipped\n\nYou always have FOUR moves, and the references below are EVIDENCE for choosing — not a mandate:\n  - ADOPT — use a reference as-is when it is an EXACT fit.\n  - ADAPT — keep a reference's pattern, override the specifics, when it mostly fits.\n  - SPECIALIZE — mint a CHILD of the nearest reference (`mint-behavior!` with `:parent <that behavior-id>`) when the top hit is a BROAD shape rather than an exact fit; the child keeps the proven shape, pins your domain, and accrues evidence under the parent. This is the RECOMMENDED move when a match cleared threshold only on shape.\n  - MINT — mint a fresh behavior (`:parent nil`) when the task is genuinely novel and no reference is a true parent.\nA match clearing threshold does NOT mean adopt/adapt is your only option — weigh each reference's strengths AND its `:avoid-when` against THIS task, then pick the right move. Your job is the RIGHT tree for THIS task; the corpus is evidence, not gospel.\n\n### Structural patterns (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n#### Top match — Draft + critique (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: This task is a high-priority diagnostic quality audit where the user needs a 'grade' (bottleneck diagnosis, recommendations, improvements) on an existing artifact (SQL logs/stats). The 'Draft + critique' pattern's strength of using structured :output-schemas to force specific dimensions (diagnosis, recommendation, improvement estimate) perfectly matches the multi-part diagnostic requirements of the query. Its single-pass nature fits a diagnostic task where the human reviewer reads the grade/recommendation first.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nDraft + critique is a single-pass quality-auditing variant of validation-loop: producer LLM emits a draft, critic LLM grades it on structured dimensions, no retry. The structured :output-schemas on the critic force specific grading dimensions (score, issue list, recommendation) — prevents vague 'looks good' grades. Always use DIFFERENT models for draft vs critique to break error correlation. Use for quality auditing where a human reads the grade first; use iterative-refinement instead when the artifact itself is the output. Use producer-validator with code-based check when rules are formal.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence with a producer :llm + a critic :llm (structured grading output) — no retry budget; the critic's grade is the loop's output\n  - natural for quality auditing: you want both the artifact AND an explicit quality rating to surface to a human reviewer\n  - outputs typically include the draft, a structured grade (score + categories + specific issues), and (optionally) a revision recommendation\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit grading stage forces the system to articulate quality dimensions BEFORE a human looks at the artifact — the reviewer reads the grade first and uses it to focus their attention (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the artifact will be reviewed by a human AND specific quality categories matter (factual accuracy, tone, completeness)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:prompt] :writes [:draft]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft :prompt] :writes [:score :issues :recommendation] :output-schemas {:score :int :issues [:vector :string] :recommendation :string}}] [:final {:keys [:draft :score :issues :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** structured :output-schemas on the critic forces specific dimensions (score, issue list, recommendation) — prevents the LLM from emitting vague 'looks good' grades (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: quality has clear dimensions that can be enumerated upfront (accuracy, completeness, tone)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:writes [:score :issues :recommendation] :output-schemas {:score [:and :int [:>= 0] [:<= 10]] :issues [:vector :string] :recommendation [:enum :ship :revise :discard]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using the SAME model for draft and critique correlates errors — the model that drafted a flawed argument may grade it as fine (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: draft and critique use the same model AND the quality dimensions involve subtle judgment\n    - Recommended fix: use different models for draft vs critique (e.g., draft with gemini, critique with claude) OR add a third independent reviewer model to break ties\n\n  - **Failure mode:** no retry means the loop ships a flawed draft with a low grade rather than fixing it — fine for auditing, wrong for production output (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the loop is in a path that emits to end-users (the artifact is the output, not the grade)\n    - Recommended fix: use iterative-refinement (draft → critique → revise) when the loop is in the output path, OR add retry-on-low-grade via producer-validator semantics\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - essay grading: draft answer → grade with rubric → surface to instructor\n  - code review automation: PR diff → critique against style/correctness rules → flag for human review\n  - blog post quality check: draft post → grade for accuracy/tone/completeness → recommend ship/revise/discard\n\n#### Alternative #1 (confidence: 0.88)\nWhy this fits: The query involves multiple distinct inputs (logs, plans, stats) that must be processed to reach a final refactored SQL output. The ETL pipeline's strength in chaining sequential stages (normalization of logs, enrichment with stats, formatting of final SQL) matches the multi-step nature of the analysis. The recommendation to use :code for deterministic formatting is ideal for the 'final refactored SQL' requirement.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n#### Alternative #2 — Iterative refinement (confidence: 0.75)\nWhy this fits: The query asks for a high-quality refactored SQL result. Iterative refinement (draft -> critique -> revise) is well-suited for code optimization tasks where an initial rewrite might need a second pass to ensure it actually solves the bottlenecks identified in the critique. However, it lacks the explicit multi-input diagnostic focus of the higher-ranked candidates.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nIterative refinement is a fixed-N sequence (typically 3 stages: draft → critique → revise) where each stage transforms a single artifact toward higher quality. The critique stage forces explicit articulation of defects before the revision stage addresses them — measurably improves nuanced outputs over single-pass with self-edit. Always pass the original prompt into the revision stage so the rewrite doesn't drift. Avoid for short/concrete outputs (single :llm is enough) or for unbounded-quality scenarios (use validation-loop instead). Distinct from validation-loop: refinement is a fixed-N polish; validation-loop is producer/validator with retry.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of typically 3 stages: initial draft → critic feedback → revised draft (sometimes repeated for a fixed N)\n  - natural for content quality improvement where each pass identifies and addresses specific defects from the previous pass\n  - stages share the same content key but transform it forward — :draft → :critique → :revised-draft\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit critique stage between draft and revision forces the model to articulate specific defects before fixing them — outputs are higher quality than single-pass with self-edit (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the output requires nuance, factual precision, or stylistic care AND a single :llm pass tends to miss issues that re-reading would catch\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:prompt] :writes [:draft]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft :prompt] :writes [:critique]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft :critique :prompt] :writes [:revised-draft]}] [:final {:keys [:revised-draft]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** pass the original prompt into the revision stage's :reads alongside the critique — ensures the revision doesn't drift away from the original ask while addressing critique (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the critique stage tends to focus on style/issues and might lose sight of the original requirement\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:draft :critique :prompt] :writes [:revised-draft]}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** fixed 3-stage refinement burns 3x the tokens of a single pass — overkill when the output is short or the prompt is straightforward (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: output is under 200 tokens AND the prompt is concrete (no nuance) — single :llm with a careful instruction is enough\n    - Recommended fix: use a single :llm with an instruction that explicitly tells the model 'draft, then critique, then revise inline' — much cheaper and often comparable quality\n\n  - **Failure mode:** open-ended (unbounded N iterations until convergence) is the wrong tool here — that's validation-loop's job, not iterative-refinement's (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the loop needs to repeat until quality threshold is met OR the work needs explicit retry-on-fail semantics\n    - Recommended fix: use validation-loop (with a producer + a validator + a retry budget) for unbounded-quality scenarios; iterative-refinement is for fixed-N refinement passes\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - technical writing improvement: draft documentation → critique for clarity/missing-context → revise\n  - creative writing polish: draft scene → critique for pacing/voice → revise\n  - executive summary tightening: draft summary → critique for jargon/specificity → revise\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 5 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: The task is a classic 'explain why it is broken and fix it' loop. Bug-diagnosis is the most specific fit as it focuses on establishing a reproduction (which the execution plan and slow query log provide) and bisecting candidate causes for a minimal fix. Its strength in deriving the root cause before editing aligns perfectly with the need to diagnose the bottleneck before providing rewrites.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code is broken and fixes it — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop sharpening the Investigation parent for the code-bug case. The load-bearing traits are establishing a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix (a flaky symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger or you can't know the fix worked) and enumerating candidate causes to rule out with evidence before converging (don't fix the first plausible guess). Prefer this child of Investigation whenever the task is 'this test/behavior is broken, find why and fix it'. Avoid when building new functionality from a spec (code-building, no failure to diagnose) or doing exploratory research with no specific failure (Research).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a failing/flaky test or a wrong-behavior bug report + the relevant code, reproduce it deterministically, isolate the root cause, and produce the minimal fix\n  - natural fit when the goal is 'this code is broken, find WHY and fix it' — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop, distinct from greenfield code-building\n  - outputs are typically a deterministic repro, a root-cause statement tied to a specific code location, and the minimal change that makes the repro pass\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** establish a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix — a flaky/intermittent symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger first, or you cannot know the fix worked (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the bug is intermittent or its trigger is not yet understood (e.g. an intermittent nil from shared state / timing)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :code] :writes [:candidate-causes]}] [:code {:reads [:candidate-causes] :writes [:repro] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:repro :deterministic-failing-case})}] [:llm {:reads [:repro :code] :writes [:root-cause :fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate causes upfront and rule them out with evidence before converging — bisect the search space rather than fixing the first plausible guess (which often masks the real cause) (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes (timing, shared state, environment, off-by-one)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:hypotheses :evidence] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining] :writes [:root-cause :minimal-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** jumping straight to a code edit on the first hypothesis (a code-building instinct) without a deterministic repro produces a fix that may paper over the symptom while the real defect remains — and you cannot prove it is fixed (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom is intermittent OR the root cause is not yet evidenced\n    - Recommended fix: reproduce deterministically first, name the root cause with evidence, then make the minimal change and re-run the repro\n\n  - **Failure mode:** an over-broad 'fix' that rewrites surrounding code while chasing a bug introduces new regressions and obscures which change actually resolved the defect (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the bug is localized and a targeted change suffices\n    - Recommended fix: scope the fix to the proven root cause; keep unrelated code untouched so the repro pinpoints the effective change\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - debug why the integration test checkout-flow-test fails intermittently with a nil cart-id and make it deterministic\n  - fix the off-by-one bug in the pagination loop so the final page renders its last row instead of dropping it\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                   