of dates, and a specific entity list from a 90-page transcript.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nDocument-analysis trees extract summary + key-dates + entities from a large single-document input. The proven shape is chunked-extraction: :chunk-document → :map-each (bounded :max-concurrency 3) over per-chunk :llm → :aggregate → final synthesis :llm. Always chunk for documents above ~5KB. Always bound :max-concurrency to avoid sub-LLM rate-limit exhaustion. For independent summary + entity extraction, use :parallel to reduce wall-time.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - extracts structured information (summary, dates, entities) from a long single-document input\n  - natural fit for chunked-extraction tree shape: chunk-document → map-each → aggregate → :llm synth\n  - produces three independent output keys (summary, key-dates, entities) consumed by downstream nodes\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** chunked-extraction with bounded :max-concurrency reliably extracts entities from large documents (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input is a single document >5KB with extractable entities AND output is a structured triple (summary + dates + entities)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :size 5000 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :results :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:entities :dates] :output-schemas {:entities [:vector :string] :dates [:vector :string]}}]] [:aggregate {:from :results :writes [:entities :dates]}] [:llm {:reads [:document] :writes [:summary]}] [:final {:keys [:summary :entities :dates]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** parallel summary + entity extraction reduces wall-time when downstream nodes are independent (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the document size justifies chunking AND summary doesn't need to consume entity-extraction output\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:document] :writes [:summary]}] [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :size 5000 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :results :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:risks]}]] [:aggregate {:from :results :writes [:risks]}]]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** unbounded :max-concurrency on per-chunk :llm calls exhausts rate limits on documents with 6+ chunks (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input is a large document AND :max-concurrency is unset on the :map-each\n    - Recommended fix: always set :max-concurrency to 3 on the :map-each parent of per-chunk :llm calls\n\n  - **Failure mode:** single-shot :llm on a >50KB document either truncates or burns token budget without yielding good output (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the document is >5KB and the tree is a single :llm without chunking\n    - Recommended fix: always chunk first (use :chunk-document) for documents above ~5KB\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - large RFP-style document extraction (a real public-sector RFP, ~280K chars, is the canonical exercise — produces summary + dates + entities triple)\n  - any large structured document extraction (RFP, contract, regulatory filing)\n\n#### Alternative #1 — ParallelIndependent (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: The intent involves three distinct, independent outputs (summary, timeline, entities) derived from the same source. The recommended pattern for ParallelIndependent ('summary + entities + key-dates') mirrors the structure of this request. However, it is slightly less fit than document-analysis because it doesn't natively handle the chunking logic required for a 90-page 'beast' of a file, though it describes the output relationship well.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nParallelIndependent is a :parallel of N children where each child writes to DISTINCT keys. Wall-time equals the slowest child instead of their sum. Use when the same input needs 2-3+ independent transformations and child latencies are similar. Children CANNOT read each other's outputs; overlapping :writes keys race; the slowest child bottlenecks total time. Distinct from :map-each — :map-each iterates a COLLECTION of inputs; this iterates a SET of TRANSFORMATIONS on one input.\n\n\n#### Alternative #2 — ChunkedExtraction (confidence: 0.70)\nWhy this fits: This is the core skeleton for the task (processing a 90-page file per-chunk). It is highly relevant, but the 'document-analysis' candidate is essentially a more specialized version of this that pre-configures the specific entity/date/summary triple Alex needs.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nChunkedExtraction is the canonical 4-stage shape for processing large documents: :chunk-document → :map-each (bounded :max-concurrency 3) → :aggregate → optional final :llm synthesis. Reusable across any domain where the input is a large document and the work is per-chunk independent. Always bound :max-concurrency on the :map-each to avoid sub-LLM rate-limit exhaustion. Skip the pattern for documents <5KB or when cross-chunk reasoning is required.\n\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 4 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Extraction (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: This task is primarily a high-recall extraction effort from a long (88-page) document. The candidate's recommended pattern of using :map-each over chunks is essential here to avoid the 'token-pressure recall loss' mentioned in its weaknesses, ensuring every date and entity across the beast of a transcript is captured. The use of :output-schemas is also critical for the caller's specific requirements (dates, organizations, individuals,