he proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n#### Alternative #1 — SequentialPipeline (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: The task has explicit ordered dependencies: calculate the math first, then identify allergens, then format the label. This matches the SequentialPipeline's strength for 'explicit ordered dependencies' where each phase consumes the previous output.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nSequentialPipeline is a :sequence of 2+ :llm/:code nodes where each step consumes the previous step's output. Natural for ETL (extract → enrich → format) and iterative refinement (draft → critique → revise). Avoid when stages are independent (use :parallel) or when 3+ small :llm steps could be one larger :llm — small successive :llm calls burn tokens that a single multi-step instruction would cover.\n\n\n#### Alternative #2 — ParallelIndependent (confidence: 0.75)\nWhy this fits: The three items (Salad, Hummus, Wrap) are independent of each other and can be processed in parallel to reduce wall-time, fitting the 'SET of TRANSFORMATIONS on one input' (or in this case, a set of items) pattern.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nParallelIndependent is a :parallel of N children where each child writes to DISTINCT keys. Wall-time equals the slowest child instead of their sum. Use when the same input needs 2-3+ independent transformations and child latencies are similar. Children CANNOT read each other's outputs; overlapping :writes keys race; the slowest child bottlenecks total time. Distinct from :map-each — :map-each iterates a COLLECTION of inputs; this iterates a SET of TRANSFORMATIONS on one input.\n\n\nAssigned to domain child 26b7b5e2-8dda-386e-8248-b6c6b1b54aeb (label: nutrition-label-generation) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\nCapabilities:\n  - Domain family born for a task whose subject matter, material and output kind are: The ETL pipeline's strengths in content transformation followed by deterministic formatting (CSV, fixed-width, or in this case, packaging labels) match the task of converting raw ingredient data into consumer-ready nutrition metrics and labels.\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** emits the 1181952603 worked tree for tasks of this class (confidence 0.50, evidence-count 0)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:instruction \"Using the recipe information provided in the task description (Quinoa Power Salad, Hummus Cup, Turkey Wrap), extract the ingredients and nutritional components. Note: If specific wholesaler data wasn't provided as a separate file, use standard USDA-equivalent values for the components mentioned. Identify allergens (soy, wheat, dairy).\", :reads [], :writes [:structured_ingredients], :output-schemas {:structured_ingredients [:vector [:map [:item_name :string] [:raw_components [:vector :any]] [:allergens [:vector :string]]]]}}] [:llm {:instruction \"Calculate the per-serving nutritional stats based on the portion definitions: \\n                       - Quinoa Power Salad: 1 container (220g)\\n                       - Hummus Cup: 1 cup (with 4 carrots)\\n                       - Turkey & Swiss Wrap: 1/2 wrap.\\n                       Focus on Calories, Protein, Total Fat, and Total Carbs. Ensure math scales bulk to single serving.\", :reads [:structured_ingredients], :writes [:calculated_stats], :output-schemas {:calculated_stats [:vector [:map [:item_name :string] [:calories :number] [:protein_g :number] [:fat_g :number] [:carbs_g :number] [:allergens [:vector :string]]]]}}] [:llm {:instruction \"Generate three professional Nutrition Facts labels. Format them clearly for copy-pasting. Bold all allergens (soy, wheat, dairy, etc.). State the serving size clearly at the top.\", :reads [:calculated_stats], :writes [:result], :output-schemas {:result :string}}] [:final {:keys [:result]}]]\n      ```\n    - Reads: (none declared) · Writes: :structured_ingredients, :calculated_stats, :result · Outputs: :result — rebind these keys to your task's blackboard; the logic stays as written.\n\n  - **Trait:** emits the 790948384 worked tree for tasks of this class (confidence 0.40, evidence-count 0)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:instruction \"Extr