t a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n2. Behavioral suggestion (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: A core requirement of the task is maintaining performance under heavy write load and avoiding 'table bloat or CPU spikes' during the backfill. This candidate's focus on a 'measure-then-change' loop and the 'output-invariance constraint' aligns with the caller's need for data parity and specific performance metrics to trigger rollbacks.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Performance-optimization makes existing code faster WITHOUT changing what it produces — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop (really Investigation to find the hot path, then Code-building to optimize) under an output-invariance constraint. The load-bearing traits are profiling to locate the real bottleneck BEFORE editing (never guess from reading the code) and pinning output-invariance with a before/after comparison (a speedup that changes results is a regression). Prefer this over the broad Transformation parent whenever the task pairs a speed target with 'without changing the output': Transformation's one-shot 'reshape in :code' shape ships an unmeasured, possibly output-altering change. Avoid when the task adds new behavior (code-building) or has no correctness constraint (the Transformation parent).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given code that is too slow + a performance target + an output-invariance constraint, locate the hot path, change it for speed, and prove the output is unchanged\n  - natural fit when the task is 'X is slow, make it faster WITHOUT changing what it produces' — a measure-then-change-then-re-measu